    "rule": "The totality-of-the-circumstances approach treats basis of knowledge and veracity as closely intertwined factors that inform one commonsense, practical judgment rather than separate, independently mandatory boxes, so substantial corroboration of specific predicted future conduct an innocent bystander could not likely have guessed can compensate for a weak or unknown basis of knowledge.",
    "fullRule": "The totality-of-the-circumstances approach treats basis of knowledge and veracity as closely intertwined factors that inform one commonsense, practical judgment rather than separate, independently mandatory boxes, so substantial corroboration of specific predicted future conduct an innocent bystander could not likely have guessed can compensate for a weak or unknown basis of knowledge. A wrongly claims the two approaches reach the same result by the same method, B and D each discard corroboration's relevance in opposite, equally incorrect ways.",
