    "caseRef": "United States v. Robinson; Chimel v. California; Riley v. California; Arizona v. Gant",
    "application": "Rule: Robinson held that a custodial arrest based on probable cause is reasonable, and a full search of the person incident to it requires no further justification. It upheld opening a crumpled cigarette package found on the arrestee. Riley carved out digital data, and Gant's \"evidence of the crime of arrest\" limit applies to vehicles, not to searches of the person. Application: Hale was in custody, and the pack was a physical container on his person. Robinson's categorical rule applies, regardless of the offense. Why not the others: A demands the case-by-case showing Robinson rejected. B misstates the law for custodial traffic arrests. D imports Gant's vehicle rule into a search of the person. Policy: Robinson's categorical rule is easy for officers to apply quickly and reflects the danger and evidence-loss risks of every custodial arrest. Critics argue it lets minor arrests become a pretext for full searches.",
    "conclusion": "The best answer is C.",
    "ruleKey": "ccp-07-full-search-of-the-person-incident-to-a-custodial-arrest"
