    "caseRef": "Colorado v. Bertine; Illinois v. Lafayette",
    "application": "Rule: Inventory searches are valid without a warrant or probable cause when they follow standardized procedures, serve administrative purposes, and are not a pretext for investigation (Bertine). The decision to impound must itself follow standardized criteria. Application: The policy allowed impounding only when no licensed driver was available. One was. Departing from the policy defeats the standardized-procedures foundation, and the officer's admission confirms the investigative purpose. Why not the others: A ignores that the policy was not actually followed. C overstates the irrelevance of motive. Inventory doctrine expressly asks whether the search was a pretext, and departure from policy is objective evidence of one. D imposes an inapplicable standard. Policy: Inventories are tolerated because they serve caretaking functions. If officers could impound cars at will to search them, the exception would be a back door around the probable-cause requirement.",
