    "caseRef": "Muehler v. Mena; Michigan v. Summers",
    "application": "Rule: Summers authorizes detaining occupants during a warrant search. Muehler upheld handcuffing an occupant for the duration of a two-to-three-hour search for weapons connected to a gang, and held that questioning her about immigration status required no independent suspicion because mere police questioning is not a seizure and did not prolong the detention. Application: The search was for firearms, so handcuffs were justified for safety. The questions did not extend the detention. Muehler controls. Why not the others: A states a rule Muehler rejected. B ignores Muehler's approval of handcuffing during a lengthy weapons search. C has no basis. Policy: Muehler lets police manage dangerous searches and use the time efficiently. Critics argue that it allows officers to use detentions tied to one investigation to explore unrelated matters, such as immigration status, without any suspicion.",
    "conclusion": "The best answer is D.",
