    "caseRef": "Riley v. California; United States v. Robinson",
    "application": "Rule: Riley held that police generally need a warrant to search digital data on a phone seized incident to arrest. Riley allowed officers to seize and secure the phone to prevent destruction of evidence while seeking a warrant, and specifically noted that remote wiping can be countered by disconnecting the phone from the network or using signal-blocking bags. Case-specific exigencies may still justify warrantless searches in genuine emergencies. Application: The officer properly secured the phone. Once it was in the bag, the wiping risk was addressed, so no exigency justified searching it. A warrant was required. Why not the others: B states the general wiping justification Riley rejected. C is wrong because seizing the phone pending a warrant is permitted. D applies Robinson's rule for physical items, which Riley refused to extend to digital data. Policy: Riley reconciles evidence preservation with privacy: officers can freeze the evidence while a neutral magistrate decides whether its contents may be searched.",
