    "caseRef": "United States v. Ross; California v. Acevedo; Carroll v. United States",
    "application": "Rule: Under Ross, when officers have probable cause to search a lawfully stopped vehicle, they may search every part of it and its contents that may conceal the object of the search, as a magistrate could authorize by warrant. Acevedo confirmed one rule for containers in cars. Application: The probable cause covered the whole SUV and concerned coins, which could fit in the toolbox. Being locked does not take the toolbox outside the search; a warrant for the vehicle would also cover it. Why not the others: B states a rule Ross rejected. C contradicts Ross. D adds an arrest requirement the automobile exception does not have. Policy: Ross matches the scope of warrantless vehicle searches to what a warrant could authorize, so the exception neither expands nor shrinks what probable cause supports. Critics argue it gives officers broad power to open closed containers holding private items without judicial review.",
